CAT = "rsracPerformance"; SEG = "PRF"
Q = [
("Return air is 24 C and supply air leaving a cooling coil is 13 C. What is the temperature split?",
 "11 K", ["37 K", "13 K", "24 K"],
 "Temperature split, or delta T, is return minus supply: 24 - 13 = 11 K. It is compared with the expected range for the humidity and airflow."),
("A chiller leaves water at 7 C while its evaporator saturation is 5 C. What is the approach temperature?",
 "2 K", ["12 K", "5 K", "7 K"],
 "Evaporator approach is leaving water temperature minus refrigerant saturation temperature: 7 - 5 = 2 K. A rising approach over time points to fouling or low charge."),
("An air-cooled condenser's condensing temperature runs much further above outdoor air than at start-up. What does that suggest?",
 "Dirty condenser coil", ["Low refrigerant charge", "Oversized evaporator", "Light indoor load"],
 "The condenser split grows when the coil cannot reject heat: fouled fins, a failed fan or recirculating air. Undercharge usually lowers condensing pressure."),
("Which reading confirms an air handler is moving its design airflow through a duct system?",
 "External static pressure", ["Supply air humidity", "Compressor current draw", "Filter pressure drop alone"],
 "Total external static pressure, read across the fan's inlet and outlet, is plotted on the maker's fan table to estimate airflow at the blower setting."),
("What is checked to confirm a building automation system controls a rooftop unit as designed?",
 "Each point responds", ["Unit paint and labels", "Refrigerant cylinder dates", "Roof drainage"],
 "System integration testing commands each output and checks each input reading at the controller and the equipment, so schedules, setpoints and alarms work as written."),
("During performance verification, why are readings taken only after the system stabilizes?",
 "Transient readings mislead", ["Gauges need warming up", "Refrigerant changes type at start", "Codes forbid early readings"],
 "Pressures, temperatures and currents swing for several minutes after start or a load change. Stable conditions give readings that can be compared with the maker's data."),
("What does a rising temperature difference across a chiller's condenser water show, at the same load?",
 "Lower water flow", ["Increased water flow", "Cleaner condenser tubes", "Lower refrigerant charge"],
 "At a given heat rejection, less water flow means each litre picks up more heat, so the water's temperature rise increases. A clogged strainer or failing pump is suspected."),
("Why is a setpoint reset strategy added during optimization of a chilled water plant?",
 "Saves energy at part load", ["Prevents refrigerant leaks", "Raises peak capacity", "Stops coil freezing"],
 "Raising the chilled water setpoint when loads are light lets the chiller work across a smaller lift, using less energy while still meeting demand."),
("Which document is handed to the owner at the end of commissioning?",
 "Start-up report", ["Supplier price list", "Apprentice's logbook", "Unused refrigerant invoice"],
 "Commissioning documentation records settings, readings and test results so the owner and later mechanics have a baseline for the system."),
("A walk-in freezer holds -20 C but its compressor runs nearly continuously. What is checked first?",
 "Coil frost and door seals", ["Thermostat brand", "Box lighting wattage", "Oil colour in the sight glass"],
 "Long run times at a held temperature point to extra load or a coil that cannot transfer heat: frosted coils, failed defrost, leaking door gaskets or doors left open."),
("Why does dehumidification drop when an evaporator fan runs at a very high speed?",
 "Coil surface runs above the dew point", ["Air carries refrigerant away", "Moisture turns into frost", "The compressor slows down"],
 "Higher airflow warms the coil surface, so less of it is below the air's dew point and less moisture condenses. Lower airflow improves moisture removal."),
("What does verifying system integration on a heat pump with backup heat confirm?",
 "Stages work together as designed", ["The heat pump never runs", "The furnace uses no gas", "The refrigerant charge is weighed"],
 "Integration testing checks that the heat pump, backup heat and controls hand off at the right temperatures and do not fight or run at once when they should not."),
("Why is compressor discharge temperature watched during performance checks?",
 "High values break down oil", ["It sets the defrost time", "It shows indoor humidity", "Low values mean overcharge"],
 "Very high discharge temperatures carbonize oil and damage valves. Causes include high superheat, high compression ratio and non-condensables."),
("A system's suction is 300 kPa absolute and discharge 1500 kPa absolute. What is the compression ratio?",
 "5 to 1", ["1200 to 1", "1800 to 1", "4 to 1"],
 "Compression ratio uses absolute pressures: 1500 / 300 = 5. Subtracting gives 1200 and adding gives 1800, neither of which is a ratio."),
("Why is the outdoor coil of a heat pump watched during a defrost cycle test?",
 "To confirm frost clears completely", ["To check paint for blisters", "To measure line voltage", "To set the thermostat"],
 "A complete defrost clears the coil and ends on time or temperature. Partial defrosts let ice build up until airflow fails."),
("Optimizing a refrigeration rack lowers its head pressure setpoint in cool weather. What is the benefit?",
 "Less compressor energy", ["More refrigerant stored", "Higher discharge temperature", "Longer defrost cycles"],
 "Floating head pressure down as outdoor temperature allows cuts the pressure the compressors must reach, saving energy, while staying above the minimum the expansion valves need."),
]
